From e49622aed57cacf9a41846700e91fdcaa48843e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kristian <kristian@apache.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 18:37:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Read protobuf fields as the reader schema reads them

Mapping columns to writer fields by number accepted any writer field of
the same wire encoding, but returned the writer's value unconverted, so
a change between types of one encoding that protobuf allows failed
queries over records written before it: an int32 field that became a
bool, an enum that became an int32, or bytes that became a string were
decoded with the wrong Java type, a uint32 widened to int64 read as a
negative number, and an enum value that was renamed kept its old name.
Convert the writer value to the value protobuf reads from the same bytes
with the reader field, which is what parsing the record with the reader
schema yields. A map field is compatible only if its key and value are,
and a google.protobuf.Timestamp or Any field, which have column types of
their own, only maps to a field of the same message type.

Field numbers identify fields within one message type, so a record of a
message type other than the reader's is matched by name, as before.

NULL is now written to, and read from, every field that tracks presence
alike: an unset proto2 optional field and an unset oneof member decode
as NULL, as message fields and proto3 optional fields do, since the
encoder leaves them unset for NULL. A proto2 required field rejects NULL
with CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION, as the message could not be built without it.

A row decoder without columns no longer looks the reader schema up,
which can take a schema registry request.
